            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The airleakage in the house was quite reasonable, but decreasing air leakage can be a cheap and effective project.



The weatherstripping around the front door could be tightened up to make sure a complete seal is made. The back door might also have a similar issue, especially since the back porch has shifted.



There was some air leakage around the chimney of the pellet stove where it meets the wall. This can be solved with some high temperature mastic which is a putty.



The header space in the basement is another common location where air leakage occurs. Most of this area is inaccessible since there is drywall up, but the space between the joists in the mechanical room could be filled in with spray foam or foam board insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <HotWaterSystem>

When it comes time to replace your hot water heater, you might consider an instantaneous heater. I recommend an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher. Condensing heaters use PVC exhaust piping that can exit through a wall, this means the existing chimney can be eliminated and cuts down on air leakage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

There is a combustion air supply duct next to the furnace. It has a "cold well" duct on the outside to help trap the cold air from leaking into the house. Since the furnace isn't used, the combustion air duct could be closed off by turning the lever on the side to a horizontal position.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 78% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

Since you use your pellet stove as the primary heat source, there wouldn't be a savings associated with the furnace replacement but it would allow you to replace your old furnace and get a rebate at the same time. I was unable to find the exact age of the furnace from the serial number but it is expected to be the same age as the house (1987). A new furnace would also have the ability to run the fan only to circulate heat from your pellet stove around the house.



By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace 1 toilet. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



The circulation pump for municipal water could be switched out to an ECM pump. Pumps for potable water should have stainless steel flanges. The current pump uses 85 watts and runs 24/7. An ECM replacement would use about 13 watts at most, this is a savings of about &lt;b&gt;$190/year.&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings:

Electricity - $18 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $1.02 per litre 

Propane - $0.57 per litre

Wood - $350 per cord 

Pellets - $7 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Nick Walker

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: nick.walker@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
